---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioToMp3 from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioToMp3.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="m4a-to-mp3">
  <GifAudioToMp3
    client:load
    accept=".m4a,.mp4,.aac,.m4b,audio/mp4,audio/aac"
    sourceLabel="M4A"
    sourceHint="Voice Memos, GarageBand bounces, iTunes tracks, phone recordings. Drop as many as you like"
    target="mp3"
    defaultBitrate="192"
    formatNote="M4A holds AAC (or occasionally Apple Lossless). Both are decoded here and re-encoded as MP3 with LAME, the same encoder desktop tools use."
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What an M4A file actually is</h2>
    <p>
      M4A is a container, not a codec: an MPEG-4 file that holds only audio. Inside, it is almost
      always AAC, the codec Apple uses for iTunes, Voice Memos and QuickTime. Sometimes it holds Apple
      Lossless (ALAC), a truly lossless format that some people rip CDs to. This M4A to MP3 converter
      decodes both, so you do not need to know which you have.
    </p>
    <p>
      The problem with M4A is support, not quality: AAC is better than MP3 at the same bitrate. Car
      head units, gym equipment, DJ software, older Android builds, transcription services and much
      embedded hardware read only MP3.
    </p>

    <h2>Picking a bitrate</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Source</th><th>Suggested MP3 bitrate</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Voice Memo, dictation, interview</td><td>96–128 kbps, mono</td><td>Speech has little high-frequency content. Mono halves the size again</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Podcast episode</td><td>128 kbps</td><td>The industry norm. Higher is inaudible over headphones on a commute</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Music from iTunes (256 kbps AAC)</td><td>256 kbps</td><td>MP3 is less efficient, so match the number, not the perceived quality</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GarageBand bounce you will keep</td><td>320 kbps</td><td>Maximum MP3 quality. Export a WAV from GarageBand if you will edit more</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>About the quality you lose</h2>
    <p>
      AAC is already lossy. To decode it to samples and encode those as MP3 is a second generation of
      loss. The two codecs discard slightly different things, so the artefacts add up. In practice the
      effect is inaudible for speech at any sensible bitrate, and very hard to hear on music at
      192 kbps or above. Two rules: encode at or above the source bitrate, and always keep the
      original M4A, because it has only one generation of loss.
    </p>

    <h2>Converting a whole folder of Voice Memos</h2>
    <p>
      Select all files at once. The queue converts them one after another, with a progress row for
      each, and gives one ZIP at the end as well as individual downloads. There is no daily or hourly
      limit. On a modern laptop a five-minute memo converts in a few seconds.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Voice memos are the most sensitive everyday audio: therapy notes, medical appointments, legal
      calls, interviews under embargo, unpublished song ideas. An upload converter asks you to trust
      its retention policy. Here, FFmpeg compiled to WebAssembly converts the file in this browser tab,
      and the site’s Content-Security-Policy allows no third-party origin. Open the Network tab and
      convert a file: after the one-time engine download, no data moves.
    </p>

    <h2>What will not work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>.m4p files are DRM-protected iTunes Store purchases from before 2009, encrypted with FairPlay. No local tool can decode them. This one tells you so and does not make a broken file.</li>
      <li>Audiobooks with chapters: a .m4b converts, but MP3 has no equivalent chapter structure, so the chapter marks are lost and you get one long track.</li>
      <li>Video files: a .mp4 with a picture track works, and the tool takes only the audio. To keep the video, use the <a href="/video-converter">video converter</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related conversions</h2>
    <p>
      If your files are raw AAC streams and not M4A, <a href="/aac-to-mp3">AAC to MP3</a> uses the same
      engine. For an uncompressed master for an editor, use <a href="/mp3-to-wav">MP3 to WAV</a>. To
      join several memos into one file first, try the <a href="/audio-joiner">audio joiner</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
